Package: lazysite-common
Architecture: all
# Depends = engine hard requirements only (top-level `use` in the shipped
# code, per dist/config/sbom-deps.json / docs/reference/host-dependencies.md):
# Template Toolkit + MultiMarkdown are the processor core; everything else
# non-core is lazy-required. perl covers the core modules (JSON::PP,
# Digest::SHA, DB_File, ...).
Depends:
 perl,
 libtemplate-perl,
 libtext-multimarkdown-perl,
 ${perl:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
# Recommends = the packaged production runtime (SM142 FastCGI pool) and the
# lazily-loaded modules behind stock manager features (theme/layout install,
# zip download, remote includes, SMTP forms).
Recommends:
 libfcgi-perl,
 libfcgi-procmanager-perl,
 libarchive-zip-perl,
 libwww-perl,
 liburi-perl,
 libio-socket-ssl-perl,
Suggests:
 libnet-xmpp-perl,
Description: static site builder and manager - engine payload and CLI
 lazysite renders Markdown sites through Template Toolkit layouts, with a
 built-in manager UI, WebDAV/MCP publishing endpoints, forms, and auth.
 .
 This package ships the engine payload at /usr/share/lazysite (never
 touching any site tree), the `lazysite` host CLI (provision, upgrade,
 check, users, dev server), the site registry directory
 /etc/lazysite/sites.d, and the lazysite@.service systemd template unit
 that runs one persistent FastCGI pool per site. Sites are provisioned
 and upgraded from the payload AS THE SITE USER, so file ownership in
 the site tree is correct by construction.
 .
 Plain CGI remains the zero-dependency fallback; the FastCGI pool
 (libfcgi-perl, libfcgi-procmanager-perl) is the production shape.

Package: lazysite-hestia
Architecture: all
# Strict same-version lazysite-common: the domain command drives that
# package's CLI/payload and the templates encode its pool-socket
# convention - the pair must move together. sudo is the drop mechanism
# (root integrator -> site user), load-bearing for the SM139 principle.
Depends:
 lazysite-common (= ${source:Version}),
 sudo,
 ${perl:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
Description: static site builder and manager - HestiaCP integration
 lazysite renders Markdown sites through Template Toolkit layouts, with a
 built-in manager UI, WebDAV/MCP publishing endpoints, forms, and auth.
 .
 This package integrates lazysite with the HestiaCP hosting panel
 (nginx in front of Apache): Apache web domain templates for both
 runtime patterns - lazysite-cgi (plain CGI, zero extra moving parts)
 and lazysite-fcgi (persistent per-domain FastCGI pool via
 lazysite@.service) - plus lazysite-hestia-domain, the root-run
 panel-side command that onboards a Hestia domain (prepares the locked
 domain-root layout, drops to the panel user to provision the site,
 registers it for fleet upgrades, and optionally enables its pool).
 .
 It replaces the hand-run installers/hestia deploy scripts of the
 tarball distribution.

Package: lazysite-apache
Architecture: all
# Strict same-version lazysite-common: the vhost command renders templates
# that encode that package's pool-socket and auth-wrapper conventions -
# the pair must move together. apache2 is Recommends, not Depends: the
# vhost files are also useful as reference on a host that fronts lazysite
# from elsewhere.
Depends:
 lazysite-common (= ${source:Version}),
 ${perl:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
Recommends:
 apache2,
Description: static site builder and manager - plain Apache glue
 lazysite renders Markdown sites through Template Toolkit layouts, with a
 built-in manager UI, WebDAV/MCP publishing endpoints, forms, and auth.
 .
 This package wires lazysite sites into a plain (non-panel) Apache host:
 commented vhost examples for both runtime patterns - plain CGI
 (FallbackResource through the CGI auth wrapper, zero extra moving
 parts) and the per-site FastCGI pool (visitor pages proxied to
 unix:/run/lazysite/<domain>.sock via mod_proxy_fcgi, session traffic
 kept on the CGI path) - plus lazysite-apache-vhost, the root-run
 command that renders them into /etc/apache2/sites-available/ (add /
 remove; it never touches site content and prints, never runs, the
 a2enmod/a2ensite steps).
 .
 The multi-server wiring reference (Apache, nginx, Caddy, lighttpd and
 the generic front-end contract) ships in this package's doc directory.

Package: lazysite-nginx
Architecture: all
# Strict same-version lazysite-common: the vhost command renders templates
# that encode that package's pool-socket and auth-wrapper conventions -
# the pair must move together. fcgiwrap is Recommends because BOTH
# shipped patterns use it: it is the whole CGI engine for the plain-CGI
# pattern, and the FastCGI-pool pattern still routes /manager, /login,
# /dav and plugin endpoints through it (the pool is anonymous by design).
Depends:
 lazysite-common (= ${source:Version}),
 ${perl:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
Recommends:
 nginx,
 fcgiwrap,
Description: static site builder and manager - nginx glue
 lazysite renders Markdown sites through Template Toolkit layouts, with a
 built-in manager UI, WebDAV/MCP publishing endpoints, forms, and auth.
 .
 This package wires lazysite sites into an nginx host: commented
 server-block examples for both runtime patterns - plain CGI (page
 misses to the CGI auth wrapper via fcgiwrap; nginx has no
 FallbackResource, so try_files + a named location provide the
 equivalent) and the per-site FastCGI pool (RECOMMENDED on nginx:
 anonymous page traffic speaks native FastCGI to
 unix:/run/lazysite/<domain>.sock, session-bearing requests are carved
 out to the CGI auth wrapper) - plus lazysite-nginx-vhost, the root-run
 command that renders them into /etc/nginx/sites-available/ (add /
 remove; it never touches site content and prints, never runs, the
 enable/reload steps).
 .
 The multi-server wiring reference (Apache, nginx, Caddy, lighttpd and
 the generic front-end contract) ships in this package's doc directory.
